en in its light. To the unaided eye, it is just one point in a pattern. A catalog supplies its coordinates, brightness, spectrum and parallax; each is a different way of asking what that point is and where it belongs. The star does not need to look spectacular for those questions to matter.\n\n**How to find it:** Beta Pyx lies at right ascension 8 hours 40 minutes and declination 35 degrees 18 minutes south (J2000 coordinates). Those coordinates make it a much easier target from the Southern Hemisphere, where it climbs higher above the horizon. From northern locations it remains accessible in principle, but sits low in the southern sky, where haze and buildings can interfere. Look when Pyxis is well placed, and use a current sky chart or planetarium app to identify the constellation rather than relying on Beta Pyx as a signpost: at fourth magnitude, it is not among the sky\u2019s brightest guide stars. Binoculars can help separate the surrounding star pattern, though the star itself should be visible to the unaided eye under reasonably dark conditions.\n\nGive your eyes time to adjust, then find the small, quiet point. Its light began travelling toward us centuries ago. A careful measurement of that light\u2019s apparent shift across the year turns the point into a place: roughly 386 light-years away, in a sky that is still changing as Earth turns beneath it.\n\n**Source:** [SIMBAD: HIP 42515 / Beta Pyx](https://simbad.cds.unistra.fr/simbad/sim-id?Ident=HIP%2042515)"
